113:
"It is said that a lion is not afraid of anyone." Hearing this, the King had drums and other instruments beaten to test the lion cub. Even upon hearing that sound, the lion cub remained lying down just as before. The King had them beaten up to three times; on the third time, the lion cub raised its head, looked at the entire assembly, and then went back to sleep in the same manner. Then the King said, "Let us depart before this lion cub's mother arrives." While departing, he thought: "Even a lion cub born on this very day is not startled and is not afraid. When shall I too be able to live without being startled or afraid, having cut off the terror of craving and views (taṇhā-diṭṭhi)?" Taking that as an object of reflection, as he went along, he saw the wind blowing without sticking to a net that had been spread out and tied to tree branches by fishermen who had caught fish. He took that as a sign as well, thinking: "When shall I too be able to go without clinging, like this, having broken through the net of craving and views and the net of delusion (moha)?"

Thereafter, having reached the garden and sitting by the side of a pond with a stone slab, he saw paduma lotuses which, though bent by the wind and touching the water, returned to their original positions unsullied by the water when the wind calmed. He took that as a sign as well, thinking: "Just as these lotuses, though born in the water, remain unsullied by the water, when shall I too be able to remain unsullied by worldly conditions (lokadhamma), though born in the world?" That King reflected repeatedly, "One must be like the lion, the wind, and the lotus—not startled, not clinging, and unsullied." He then renounced his royal majesty, became a monk, and while developing insight (vipassanā), he realized the knowledge of Paccekabodhi (Individual Enlightenment) and uttered this verse of solemn utterance (udāna-gāthā):

"Like a lion, not startled by sounds;
Like the wind, not caught in a net;
Like a lotus, unsullied by water;
One should wander alone like a rhinoceros horn."

In that verse, "sīho" refers to four types of lions: the grass lion (tiṇa-sīha), the pale-yellow lion (paṇḍu-sīha), the black lion (kāḷa-sīha), and the maned lion (kesara-sīha). Among these four, the kesara-sīha is said to be the noblest. Here, only the kesara-sīha is intended. Wind is of many kinds, such as the east wind and so on. The lotus is also of many kinds, such as red, white, and so on. Among those winds and lotuses, any kind of wind or any kind of lotus is applicable. In that context, terror arises because of self-attachment (atta-sineha). Self-attachment is the smearing of craving (taṇhā-lepa). That craving, whether associated with views or dissociated from views...

